To add a toolbar in a fragment, you can follow these steps:

1. First, add a Toolbar widget in your fragment's layout XML file. Here's an example:

```xml
<androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
    android:id="@+id/toolbar"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
    android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
    app:popupTheme="@style/AppTheme.PopupOverlay" />
```

2. Then, in your Fragment class, you need to set the Toolbar as the ActionBar. Here's how you can do it:

```java
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar;

public class MyFragment extends Fragment {

    @Override
    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_my, container, false);

        Toolbar toolbar = (Toolbar) view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);
        ((AppCompatActivity)getActivity()).setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

        // If you want to add a title
        ((AppCompatActivity)getActivity()).getSupportActionBar().setTitle("My Fragment");

        return view;
    }
}
```

Remember, you need to cast the getActivity() to AppCompatActivity before calling the setSupportActionBar() method.

Also, if you want to add menu items to your toolbar, you need to override the onCreateOptionsMenu() method in your fragment and inflate the menu XML file.

```java
@Override
public void onCreateOptionsMenu(Menu menu, MenuInflater inflater) {
    inflater.inflate(R.menu.menu_main, menu);
    super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
}
```

Don't forget to call setHasOptionsMenu(true) in the onCreate() or onCreateView() method of your fragment.

```java
@Override
public void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
    setHasOptionsMenu(true);
}
```

This is a basic way to add a toolbar in a fragment. Depending on your needs, you might need to adjust this code.
